WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:01.310
Speaker 1: All right.

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:03.720
Let's shift gears a little bit and talk about
something else.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:10.030
Many of you have written to me and said, David,
I came in with a sort of open mind and said,

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:13.330
I want to figure out on policy.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.860
Do I agree more with Democrats or Republicans?

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:16.860
Great approach.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.280
I think that that's a worthy thing to do.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.119
But then many of you who write to me say.

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:25.060
It doesn't really seem like Republicans talk
about policy anymore.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.060
Like.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:27.060
Yeah.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.860
If you look at a campaign website, you'll
see a policy section for a Republican and

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.399
it'll include a lot of buzzwords and seemingly
kind of like focus group paragraphs that don't

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:37.890
really say very much.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.360
But when it comes to the campaigns that they
run, Republicans don't really seem to talk

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.680
about policy anymore.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.130
And that's exactly what I want to talk about
today.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:51.420
I want to talk about how the Republican Party
has completely abandoned policy.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.149
It started before the Trump era, but acutely
since since the Trump era, to the point where

00:00:56.149 --> 00:01:00.370
now, instead of actually talking about issues
that affect Americans lives, the Republican

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:06.350
Party has become focused on grievances, fear
based politics, based on lies about what Democrats

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:12.970
are immigrants or others will do, and a resurgence
in extremist social views focused primarily

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:15.520
on abortion and anti-trans recently.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.280
Now, let's start with a recent example.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.090
After the 2020 election, Republicans refused
to accept the results of the election, claiming

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.740
that there was widespread fraud.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:30.030
They failed to win the hearts and minds of
the voters based on policy and when they lost.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.979
Instead of saying, Hey, we're going to put
forward a policy set of ideas, the pillars

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:37.240
that will be helpful to us in 2022.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.610
They spent two years and now it's been more
than two years claiming that that election

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:44.680
actually was rigged, that it was won by Donald
Trump and not by Joe Biden.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:51.280
And despite multiple court rulings and recount
audits, they continue to peddle that lie.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.560
And it is a way that they make voters afraid.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:01.259
They make voters afraid and angry, which are
the main emotions at this point that are being

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:05.369
utilized by Republicans to try to convince
people to come to their side.

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:10.800
And that is at the core of so much of the
rhetoric that comes from the modern Republican

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.800
Party.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:16.390
Another example is the focus on extremist
social views.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:21.890
Republicans have now become fixated on pushing
anti-transgender legislation in multiple states,

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:27.879
hundreds of bills arguing we need to protect
women's sports and we need to protect kids

00:02:27.879 --> 00:02:31.560
in bathrooms and we need to protect the integrity
of.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.680
But I'd rather not wait.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:39.459
Two of we can't have drag shows because it's
bad and they're obsessed with that stuff.

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:47.680
All of it is based on false premises, like
trans people are pedophiles or sexual predators.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.600
There aren't already laws to protect against
harassment in bathrooms when in fact they

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:57.300
are the reality that the concern, even around
sports, is a slice of a slice of a slice of

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.020
a slice of all that is going on within sports.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:06.860
And it's all about making people afraid and
angry that people like, for example, trans

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:12.780
women who they say are really men are going
to take they're either going to take your

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:18.670
child's innocence or take your child's sports
trophy or whatever the case may be.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:19.830
Fear.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.000
Anger.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.680
That's the person who I should be angry at
on immigration.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:32.631
It's the fear of supposedly lazy brown people
leeching off of social welfare programs, but

00:03:32.631 --> 00:03:34.780
also taking all of our jobs.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.500
And that's always a funny one.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:42.330
These insanely lazy people who are willing
to abandon their families and everything they

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:48.730
know, risk arrest and death to come to the
United States are then going to come here

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.980
and work in jobs that you would actually be
working.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.180
That's very confusing, but it doesn't matter.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:59.090
It's about the fear, as Tucker Carlson has
said, of people coming into the country from

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:04.329
Central and South America, making the country
dirtier and poorer and taking jobs and being

00:04:04.329 --> 00:04:06.750
on social welfare and whatever the case may
be.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.120
It's an insane juxtaposition.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.920
Lazy welfare recipients who take our jobs.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:20.040
But it is about making you afraid and angry
at those people rather than at the very corporations

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:26.060
that have lobbied against regulations which
would actually punish them more severely if

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.310
they hire undocumented immigrants.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.300
And on and on and on.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.289
Republicans have also been obsessed with cancel
culture and perceived threats to free speech.

00:04:34.289 --> 00:04:35.919
And again, it's all about fear and anger.

00:04:35.919 --> 00:04:40.370
They're going to take away your ability to
even speak at a school board meeting.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:45.789
Are they or are they going to ask me to stop
when I've ranted for 12 minutes about masks

00:04:45.789 --> 00:04:47.330
and I'm not letting anybody else speak?

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.330
Well, it is true.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:56.570
I've said before, sometimes there can be an
overreaction to someone's speech, but for

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:02.440
the most part, the issue of cancel culture
has been blown completely out of proportion

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.980
by the Republican Party to distract from important
policy discussions.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:12.509
I'm not going to redo my segment on do the
victims of cancel culture actually end up

00:05:12.509 --> 00:05:13.509
canceled?

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:14.509
The answer is no.

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:15.509
I've already talked about that.

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:23.230
But the reason for the focus on all of these
fear and anger based elements is really easy.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:27.699
You know, sometimes there's questions in politics
which are very difficult answers.

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:32.900
The answer for why Republicans have abandoned
policy is because Republicans are losing most

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.690
policy battles when it comes to the American
public.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:41.870
The American public is as against most of
what were the Republican policies today than

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:45.389
they have been for a period of decades and
decades and decades.

00:05:45.389 --> 00:05:46.650
There's so many examples of this.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:51.139
A majority of Americans support measures like
universal background checks for any gun purchase,

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:55.669
even in between private people, gun show,
whatever the case may be.

00:05:55.669 --> 00:05:58.210
Most Americans support raising the minimum
wage.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.740
Most Americans support addressing climate
change.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.220
Exactly how differs.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:05.819
But most Americans support doing something
about climate change.

00:06:05.819 --> 00:06:08.770
Most Americans support abortion being legal
in most cases.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:10.830
Most Americans now support gay marriage.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:16.180
Most Americans support using taxes on the
very rich to reduce income inequality.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:22.720
Those are all positions espoused only by Democrats
for the most part, with a few exceptions,

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.139
in the Republican Party.

00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:27.919
So when you look at that and you realize why
Republicans are opposed to all of that, or

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:33.009
at least they were when they used to talk
about policy, all they can do is focus on

00:06:33.009 --> 00:06:35.650
grievances, fear and anger.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:40.800
And that brings me to the last part of this,
which is populist rhetoric.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.880
I've explained to you before that populist
rhetoric is not a set of policy ideas.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.970
It's not like, oh, populism means a higher
minimum wage.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:52.550
Well, it might or it might mean keeping brown
people out of the country.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:53.550
Right.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:07:00.530
Populist rhetoric is often used to pull people
in and then you dump the policy on them.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.760
And the policy can differ dramatically.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.419
The populist rhetoric is a tool that politicians
use to rally supporters.

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:11.590
And usually there is some bad actor, right?

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:15.030
Is the bad actor corporations or is the bad
actor Antifa?

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.610
Well, I don't know.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:19.569
Depends on what sort of point you're trying
to make.

00:07:19.569 --> 00:07:23.759
And populist rhetoric can be used to pull
people into your movement without actually

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:25.410
talking about policy.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:31.720
The Republican Party has been particularly
adept at using populist rhetoric to appeal

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:38.330
to its base, and much of that populist rhetoric
uses fear and anger rather than actual proposed

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:39.540
policy solutions.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:45.919
And so the totality of this is that we've
seen a dangerous shift away from any substantive

00:07:45.919 --> 00:07:53.360
policy from Republicans and instead towards
increasingly extremist views on social issues

00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:00.400
and focus on grievances and a focus on fear
based politics and scapegoating.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:06.720
And at the core, it is driven by the fact
that they have lost the country on average.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.910
Can you find anti-gay-marriage people?

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:09.990
Of course you can.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:11.770
Can you find anti-abortion people?

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:12.770
Absolutely.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:13.770
There's a ton of them.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:18.660
But at the top level, it's a country that
has continued to move to the left Akin.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.810
Comically and socially Republicans have lost
that.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:25.460
And instead of saying maybe we'll revisit
our policies, maybe we will.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:29.759
No, we will use populist rhetoric to make
people afraid and angry.

00:08:29.759 --> 00:08:35.029
We won't talk about policy, and when we lose
will claim we won and try to steal the election

00:08:35.029 --> 00:08:36.450
anyway.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:38.570
Extraordinarily dangerous.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:40.400
But it's where we are now as we approach mid
2023.

